{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789956181,"nonce":"aiusTzjFONvx1wUYF0uDACGrKV4=","message":"Root cause: `LLVMBackend::compile_osr_helper` compiled the region's blocks in `HirFunction.blocks` insertion order, while the normal LLVM compile walks reverse postorder. Every `Instruction` value is pre-seeded in the value map as a zero constant, so a block that reads a value defined in a block inserted later reads the zero and codegen carries on silently.\n\nIn branchy the interpreter frame leaves into the outlined resume point `lua$main$1$resume0` (Cranelift), whose loop then asks for promotion; the region is recompiled by LLVM and its helper `osr$lua$main$1$resume0$\u003csite\u003e` (2 live-ins: acc, counter) is published, and the Cranelift region transfers into it mid-loop. In that region the inlined `zb_mod_i64` places the block defining `k` (`%v20 = sub %v11, %v47`, block25) after the if-chain blocks (block4..block8) that compare `k` against 1..5. Insertion order compiled block4 first, so every `k == n` test compared 0 against n and the chain took the `else` arm from the transfer onward. The frame layout, offsets and calling convention were all correct.\n\nPartition (NUC, x86_64, ZYLUA_LLVM=1, each 3 runs vs lua5.4):\n- branchy.lua: 466634 / 593041 / 282114 vs 140 (wrong, varies)\n- a_nomod (`//` and `-` instead of `%`, same 7-way chain): 283111 / 269195 / 665210 vs 140 (wrong)\n- b_modonly (`%`, no if-chain): 917502 (ok)\n- c_plain (`\u0026`, no if-chain): 415233 (ok)\n- d_ifonly (7-way chain on `acc \u0026 7`, no `%`): 163284 (ok: `k` is defined in the header's successor before the chain)\n- e_twoif (`%` and a two-way if): 47890 (ok: the only test on `k` sits in the block that defines it)\n- f_sum (acc depends on the counter, `%`): 1770 (ok)\n\nThe result varies per run because the wrong arm is taken only from the transfer onward, and the iteration at which the Cranelift region transfers into the LLVM helper depends on when the background compile finishes.\n\nWhy arm64 macOS was fine: with the trace on, the Mac emits the region helper but never publishes it before the loop ends (no `llvm helper ... -\u003e` / `FIRST TRANSFER` lines): the 20M-iteration loop finishes in the Cranelift region before the LLVM install completes, so the helper is never entered. Same mechanism would fire there with a longer loop.\n\nFix: `osr::blocks_reachable_from_in_rpo` (reverse postorder from the header, walking terminators like `blocks_reachable_from`), and `compile_osr_helper` compiles the region in that order, as the Cranelift helper already does through `compute_block_order`.","files":null}]}